estly favorable midterm environment gives Jones his only realistic opening, but raters and prediction markets alike see little chance of an upset in this open-seat race.", "watch": "A strong debate moment for Jones or a sharp national Democratic wave could tighten the race, but closing a double-digit gap would require a shift well beyond anything current polling shows.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Kay Ivey (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Alabama's governorship is open as Republican Kay Ivey terms out, setting up a familiar rematch: Senator Tommy Tuberville, the Republican, against former Senator Doug Jones, the Democrat, whom Tuberville defeated by roughly twenty points in 2020. The state's strong Republican lean frames the race from the start, and Tuberville enters the final weeks with a large financial advantage, having raised about three times as much as Jones.\n\nRecent polling shows Tuberville ahead by eight to thirteen points, and prediction markets give Jones roughly a six to twelve percent chance. Most professional raters call the race Solid or Safe Republican, with one at Likely Republican. Jones has campaigned actively on crime, schools, and economic issues, while Republicans have unified behind Tuberville after a residency challenge failed in state court. The race appears settled unless the political environment shifts sharply in the campaign's final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0254, "margin": -12.37}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0512, "margin": -12.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0161, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0141, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0131, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0129, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0153, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0396, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0389, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0324, "mar